All the information I will take will be taken from the official documentation https://portswigger.net/burp/documentation


🧭 Navigation & Core Components

The documentation is organized into several main modules depending on your target tool or deployment method:
Desktop Editions (Professional & Community):
  Burp Proxy: Intercept, view, and modify HTTP/S and WebSocket traffic on the fly.
  Burp Repeater: Craft, manipulate, and replay individual HTTP requests to test application logic.
  Burp Intruder: Automate customized, high-volume attacks for fuzzing, brute-forcing, and enumeration.
  Burp Target: Analyze the attack surface, manage site maps, and define target scopes.
  Other Built-in Tools: Includes Sequencer, Decoder, Comparer, DOM Invader, Organizer, and Logger.
  Burp Scanner: Automated crawling and auditing engine for detecting web vulnerabilities.
  Burp Suite DAST: Enterprise-enabled dynamic web vulnerability scanner.
  Burp Collaborator: External service integration network for detecting out-of-band/blind vulnerabilities.
  AI Features & Burp AT: Guides on leveraging AI agents and automated features to assist human-led penetration testing.
